%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: ad496817cc2ac7b498b32f0fc2de6fb3, type: 3}
  m_Name: R1_05_LongPush
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::JournalEntry
  title: "5 \xB7 Long Push"
  pages:
  - bigIdea: Friction pushes back against sliding. How hard depends on the surface
      and on how hard it's pressed.
    notes: "The old causeway is three floors in one: dry stone, slick wet stone and
      thick moss. Same crate, same weight, very different grip. The surface's roughness
      is a number called \u03BC (\"mu\")."
    lookCloser: "Friction f = \u03BC \xD7 N, and on a flat floor N = m\xB7g = 2 \xD7
      29.43 = <color=#8a5a2e>58.9 N</color>\nStone \u03BC = 1/3 \u2192 <color=#7d3029>19.6
      N</color>    Wet stone \u03BC = 0.05 \u2192 <color=#7d3029>2.9 N</color>\nMoss
      \u03BC = 0.6 \u2192 <color=#7d3029>35.3 N</color>   (one push is <color=#a26029>12
      N</color>, the tether <color=#267682>24 N</color>)"
    funFact: "Curling players sweep the ice in front of the stone: a thin layer of
      melt lowers \u03BC, so the stone slides farther."
    marginP1: On the wet stone I can push it alone!
    marginP2: On the moss, not even both of us.
    diagram:
      show: 1
      objectLabel: 'Crate

        2 kg'
      forces:
      - label: Push
        newtons: 12
        direction: 0
        kind: 0
        unit: N
      - label: Friction
        newtons: 19.6
        direction: 1
        kind: 2
        unit: N
      caption: 'On stone: 12 N can''t beat 19.6 N of friction.'
    sketch: {fileID: 0}
  - bigIdea: Less friction, longer slide. Friction is what stops a sliding thing.
    notes: A Burst gives any 2 kg crate the same speed. After that, only friction
      slows it down, so the floor decides how far it goes. On wet stone it barely
      slows at all; on moss it stops almost at once.
    lookCloser: "Burst: \u0394v = <color=#896923>7.5 m/s</color>. Stopping distance
      d = v\xB2 \xF7 (2\xB7\u03BC\xB7g):\nStone \u2248 2.9 m    Wet stone \u2248
      19 m    Moss \u2248 1.6 m\n(Real surfaces grip a little harder to start than
      to slide; this world uses one \u03BC for both.)"
    funFact: "On dry roads car tyres have \u03BC close to 1. On ice it can drop below
      0.1, which is why braking distances explode."
    marginP1: One kick and it's gone!
    marginP2: And the moss is the brake.
    diagram:
      show: 1
      objectLabel: 'Crate

        2 kg'
      forces:
      - label: Friction
        newtons: 2.9
        direction: 1
        kind: 2
        unit: N
      caption: 'On wet stone: only 2.9 N slows it down.'
    sketch: {fileID: 0}
  - bigIdea: Same push, different crate. A Burst gives every crate the same momentum,
      so the light one leaves twice as fast.
    notes: 'The old gate wants 80 N on the moss, so both crates must rest there:
      58.9 + 29.4 = 88 N. But burst the light crate and it shoots straight across
      the moss. It needs a gentler start: run it along and let it go.'
    lookCloser: "\u0394v = J \xF7 m:  2 kg \u2192 <color=#896923>7.5 m/s</color>   
      1 kg \u2192 <color=#896923>15 m/s</color>\nFriction slows ANY crate by \u03BC\xB7g
      each second (moss: 17.7 m/s every second).\nTwice the speed \u2192 four times
      the distance: on moss 1.6 m vs 6.4 m."
    funFact: Stopping distance grows with the square of speed. That's why driving
      twice as fast needs about four times the room to stop.
    marginP1: Gently?? I only know one setting.
    marginP2: Then I'll push this one. You kick the heavy one.
    diagram:
      show: 1
      objectLabel: 'Crate

        1 kg'
      forces:
      - label: Burst
        newtons: 15
        direction: 1
        kind: 5
        unit: "N\xB7s"
      caption: "Same impulse, half the mass: \u0394v = 15 \xF7 1 = 15 m/s."
    sketch: {fileID: 0}
  tip: 'Both crates must end on the moss. Run the light one along and let it glide;
    a Burst sends it flying past. Nobody can get behind the heavy one in the alcove:
    pull it out first, then Burst it west. The moss will catch it.'
  sketch: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 3f81ee8c2b54d224fadbd090b196b090, type: 3}
  sketchCaption: 'Arrows: what powers what.'
